session "Directed_Cycle_DFS" = "Directed_Set_Graphs" +
  description \<open>Directed-cycle detection by depth-first search.

    The session first exposes the generic DFS skeleton (DFS_Skeleton) and the directed-cycle
    detector built on it (DFS_DirCycle) as a minimal, light session, so that both can be reused by
    external developments -- e.g. a datalog foundedness check -- without the heavy
    Graph_Algorithms_Dev dependencies (Verified_SAT_Based_AI_Planning, BFS, Bellman-Ford, ...).

    On top of that it develops Graph_Topological_Order -- a finite directed graph has a topological
    numbering iff it has no directed cycle, which is the usual way to certify acyclicity -- and a
    whole-graph (linear) directed-cycle search, the directed counterpart of DFS_Cycles. The search
    is developed as a chain of refinements, each level removing one set operation from a hot loop
    and each justified by an equivalence back to the level below it. Every level ships an inner
    pre-seeded DFS (..._Aux) and an outer sweep that calls it once per remaining root.

      * Level 0 -- DFS_DirCycle_Linear{_Aux}, on DFS_Skeleton and DFS_DirCycle unchanged. The
        reference implementation: the back-edge test recomputes "seen - finished" at every skeleton
        step, and the outer loop recomputes "V - finished" at every iteration.
      * Level 1 -- DFS_Skel_More, which duplicates DFS_Skeleton solely to add an on_push callback,
        and DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked{_Aux} on top of it. The gray (on-stack) and unfinished sets
        become state components maintained incrementally, so both of level 0's differences
        disappear.
      * Level 2 -- DFS_Skel_More_Refine, DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked_Aux_Refine and
        DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked_Refine on top of it, where the adjacency map itself moves into
        the state and on_push deletes the pushed vertex's in-edges (via a static reverse adjacency
        map fixed by the instance, so the deletions cost one pass over the edges in total). The
        remaining difference, "neighbourhood - seen" (evaluated twice per step), goes too: the loop
        reads a plain neighbourhood. The outer sweep threads that pruned map from one inner call to
        the next, so the pruning is done once over the whole run rather than once per root; its
        state record extends level 1's, and the equivalence back to level 1 is a plain equation
        under the record package's truncate, from which soundness and completeness transport. The
        inner search needs one assumption the vset ADT does not supply, sel_cong -- that sel is
        determined by the element set -- because the two levels hand sel vsets built by different
        operations; the outer sweep does not, since it selects from the very vset level 1 selects
        from.\<close>
  theories
    DFS_Skeleton
    DFS_DirCycle
    Graph_Topological_Order
    DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Aux
    DFS_DirCycle_Linear
    DFS_Skel_More
    DFS_Skel_More_Refine
    DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked_Aux
    DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked
    DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked_Equiv
    DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked_Aux_Refine
    DFS_DirCycle_Linear_Tracked_Refine
